WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:05.500
Pictures are one of the most incredible inventions.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.830
Snapshots in time of people, places, and events—frozen
forever, along with all of those special memories

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.540
and feelings attached.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.580
They can convey emotion, illustrate ideas,
and even inspire the human spirit.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.380
But we mostly use them to take pictures of
our food.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.569
Let’s take a look at some interesting facts
you may not know about one of the most popular

00:00:24.569 --> 00:00:33.130
photography and social networking apps out
there: Instagram.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:38.090
The computer code powering the social media
powerhouse known as Instagram was written

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:43.440
over a period of eight weeks by cofounder
Kevin Systrom, though he says that over a

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.850
year of work went into the final product.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:50.550
He taught himself the art of computer programming
in the late hours after his day job, developing

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.890
a few basic ideas that helped him to hone
his programming skills whilst laying down

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:57.660
the groundwork for the app we know and love.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:02.179
The name "Instagram" is a mash up of "instant"
and "telegram".

00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:08.190
It was inspired by the way snapshots are taken
with the app and sent "over the wire" to others.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:13.010
Sure, it’s not technically accurate, but
it’s a lot catchier than “Insta-teleport

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:15.121
your pictures through the air”.

00:01:15.121 --> 00:01:19.660
The popular trend of making nostalgic posts
to social media known as "Throwback Thursday"

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.300
first began on Instagram.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.640
So remember to thank Instagram the next time
your friends share that old embarrassing photo

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:26.640
of you.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.340
The app was initially released on October
6th, 2010.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:35.770
It figuratively exploded overnight, gathering
over one million registered users by the end

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:36.770
of the year.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:41.720
By December of 2011, Apple named Instagram
their App of the Year, and the world of hastily

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:47.080
posted meal photography with a filter slapped
on top hasn’t been the same since.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:52.080
Former wrestler and Hollywood superstar Dwayne
“The Rock” Johnson organized a crowd of

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.060
fans at a movie premier in London in order
to break the world record for most selfies

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.360
taken in three minutes, as recognized by the
Guinness Book of World records.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:07.440
In November of 2010, the Instagram team made
the decision to move into an empty office

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.690
in San Francisco’s South Park.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:15.470
But it wasn’t just any old office, it was
the former first office of another social

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.560
media powerhouse known as Twitter.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.600
Hot off their first month of resounding success,
the fledgling team was eager to make a statement

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.390
to the rest of Silicon Valley: they were ready
to join the big leagues.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:34.000
Popular musician Ellie Goulding created an
entire music video for her song "Anything

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.510
Could Happen" using nothing but fan submitted
Instagram photos.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:42.930
The video features twelve hundred photos stitched
together with some snazzy editing for the

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:48.319
entire three minute and fifty-one second runtime,
immortalising her lucky fans for as long as

00:02:48.319 --> 00:02:50.030
YouTube persists.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:56.490
Equal parts creepy and cool, Instagram allows
you to tag your current geographical location

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.330
when you snap a selfie.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:03.160
Using their Photo Map feature, you can see
every place you and you friends have taken

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.610
pictures, for better or worse.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:09.850
Just remember not to tag your location when
you’ve just called in sick to work but you’re

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:13.310
actually on an impromptu camping trip with
your mates.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:19.150
In 2012, Facebook purchased Instagram for
an estimated one billion US dollars in cold

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.550
hard cash and company stocks.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:29.770
Instagram’s Twitter account has made 6,497
posts for its 39.6 million followers.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:37.170
It only follows three users in turn: Instagram
@music and the two cofounders of the company.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:42.180
However, even with that many followers it
lands only 12th on the list of the most followed

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.730
Twitter accounts, behind the likes of Barack
Obama, Youtube, and Twitter itself.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:53.209
One of Instagram’s early prototypes was
titled “Burbn” named after one of cofounder

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:55.680
Kevin Systrom’s favorite drinks.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:01.060
The most popular filter on Instagram is actually
no filter at all!

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.230
Sometimes a good picture just speaks for itself.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:07.319
Other times, you really should have dressed
it up at least a little.

00:04:07.319 --> 00:04:10.670
The line between those two worlds is perilously
thin, my friends.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:16.620
As of January 2015, Instagram sees traffic
from over three hundred million monthly active

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:17.620
users.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.609
That’s a whole lot of people chronicling
the minutiae of their lives with the humble

00:04:21.609 --> 00:04:22.889
photography app, falling just shy of the total
population of the United States.

00:04:22.889 --> 00:04:29.069
According to a study performed by AT&amp;T, a
staggering 70% of drivers admit to using their

00:04:29.069 --> 00:04:30.810
phones whilst driving.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:36.599
From that group of naughty drivers, 14% admit
to have been using Instagram.

00:04:36.599 --> 00:04:41.539
Who knows, in future public awareness advertisements
might be telling you not the add filters to

00:04:41.539 --> 00:04:43.080
selfies and drive.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:48.810
The first Instagram photo was uploaded on
July 16th, 2010, whilst the app was still

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:50.199
in development.

00:04:50.199 --> 00:04:56.340
Two short years after the first test picture
was uploaded, over one billion user-shared-photos

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.509
had joined it in the cloud.

00:04:58.509 --> 00:05:03.650
The most followed account on Instagram is,
unsurprisingly, Instagram itself.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:08.430
Next in line is the ever-popular singer and
actress Beyoncé, with almost forty million

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.650
followers as of July 2015.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:17.349
Following close behind is the no less influential
but perhaps far more controversial Kim Kardashian,

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:20.830
with around thirty nine million followers.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:25.909
In a study which included many Instagram sourced
photos, it was determined that filtered photographs

00:05:25.909 --> 00:05:32.979
are 21% more likely to be viewed and 45% more
likely to be commented on by viewers.

00:05:32.979 --> 00:05:37.350
The spike in engagement was most noteworthy
with filters that boosted the picture’s

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.960
warmth, exposure, and contrast.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:45.770
Back in 2012, when Instagram was managed by
just fifteen employees and still surging with

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:51.150
bewildering popularity, certain unsavory types
began to use it as a platform for sharing

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.180
x-rated images.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.249
Since then, the development team has worked
hard to tone down the titillating content

00:05:57.249 --> 00:05:59.970
in favor of more family friendly fare.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.150
But have they succeeded?

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:07.349
In January of 2015, an Instagram user had
their account banned for showing a little

00:06:07.349 --> 00:06:09.569
too much besides their bikini.

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:12.779
To put it bluntly her pubic hair was on show.

00:06:12.779 --> 00:06:17.509
Instagram reversed their decision in short
order, after reviewing the case and determining

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:23.189
that there was no actual nudity on display,
but a staunch debate continues to rage on

00:06:23.189 --> 00:06:28.750
to this day between Instagram and the feminist
community, over what should be considered

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:49.909
censorable material.

